Lab 17: Work with the Automation Controller API
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Explain the fundamentals of the Automation Controller REST API and its authentication model
Use the API via curl to launch and monitor a job template execution
Write a Python API client that launches jobs, checks job status, and lists job templates
Generate a simple job execution summary report from API data
Apply basic API security practices (token handling, SSL verification) and troubleshoot common API errors
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Ansible and Ansible Automation Platform concepts (job templates, inventories, organizations)
Familiarity with REST API concepts and HTTP methods (GET, POST)
Basic Python programming knowledge
Understanding of JSON data format
Access to an Automation Controller instance (or Ansible Automation Platform trial/demo instance) with at least one existing job template (for example, the built-in "Demo Job Template")
This lab assumes you are working in an isolated, self-owned or sanctioned practice environment (such as an Al Nafi ready-to-use cloud machine) with Automation Controller already installed. All API calls in this lab are read-only or act only on test resources you create yourself.

Install the required Python library before starting Task 2:

pip3 install --user requests
Verify the tools you will use are available:

curl --version
jq --version
python3 --version
Lab Environment
Your lab environment includes:

Ansible Automation Platform with Automation Controller reachable at a hostname or IP you control
Python 3.x with the requests library installed
curl and jq utilities
A text editor (vim or nano)
Throughout this lab, replace your-controller-hostname with your actual controller hostname or IP address.

Task 1: Launch and Monitor a Job Using the API with curl
Subtask 1.1: Set Up API Authentication
Create a working directory for the lab:

mkdir -p ~/api-lab
cd ~/api-lab
Create an API token through the Automation Controller web interface:

Log into Automation Controller
Navigate to Users -> your username -> Tokens
Click Add, leave scope as Write, and click Save
Copy the token value shown (it is displayed only once)
Store your connection details as environment variables in a credentials file:

cat > ~/.api_credentials << 'EOF'
export CONTROLLER_HOST="your-controller-hostname"
export CONTROLLER_TOKEN="your-api-token-here"
EOF

chmod 600 ~/.api_credentials
source ~/.api_credentials
Note on SSL verification: the examples in this lab use the curl -k flag and the Python verify=False option because a typical lab Automation Controller instance uses a self-signed certificate. The -k flag disables SSL certificate verification, which is acceptable for an isolated practice environment but is not safe for production. In a production environment, use a proper CA-signed certificate and verify against it instead of disabling verification, for example:

curl --cacert /path/to/cert.pem \
     -H "Authorization: Bearer $CONTROLLER_TOKEN" \
     https://$CONTROLLER_HOST/api/v2/job_templates/
Deliverable for this subtask: a working ~/.api_credentials file (permissions 600) and confirmation that echo $CONTROLLER_HOST and echo $CONTROLLER_TOKEN print your values after sourcing the file.

Subtask 1.2: Explore the API and List Job Templates
Confirm you can reach the API root:

curl -k -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $CONTROLLER_TOKEN" \
     https://$CONTROLLER_HOST/api/v2/ping/ | jq '.'
Expected output: a JSON object containing an "active_node" field and a "version" field, confirming the controller is reachable and your token is valid.

List available job templates:

curl -k -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $CONTROLLER_TOKEN" \
     https://$CONTROLLER_HOST/api/v2/job_templates/ | jq '.results[] | {id, name}'
Expected output: a list of JSON objects, each showing an id and name, for example {"id": 7, "name": "Demo Job Template"}. Note the id of the template you will launch next (the built-in "Demo Job Template" is used in the rest of this lab).

Deliverable for this subtask: the JSON output listing at least one job template's id and name.

Subtask 1.3: Launch a Job Using curl
Look up the job template ID by name and store it in a variable:

JOB_TEMPLATE_ID=$(curl -k -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $CONTROLLER_TOKEN" \
                  https://$CONTROLLER_HOST/api/v2/job_templates/ | \
                  jq -r '.results[] | select(.name=="Demo Job Template") | .id')

echo "Job Template ID: $JOB_TEMPLATE_ID"
If this prints nothing, list the templates again and substitute the exact name of a template that exists in your environment.

Launch the job template:

JOB_RESPONSE=$(curl -k -s -X POST \
               -H "Authorization: Bearer $CONTROLLER_TOKEN" \
               -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
               https://$CONTROLLER_HOST/api/v2/job_templates/$JOB_TEMPLATE_ID/launch/)

echo "$JOB_RESPONSE" | jq '.'

JOB_ID=$(echo "$JOB_RESPONSE" | jq -r '.job')
echo "Launched Job ID: $JOB_ID"
Note that the launch/ endpoint response contains a "job" field with the numeric job ID (not "id" at the top level in most Controller versions), which is why the script extracts .job.

Deliverable for this subtask: a non-empty $JOB_ID value printed to the terminal, corresponding to a new entry visible under Jobs in the Automation Controller web interface.

Subtask 1.4: Monitor Job Execution
Create a monitoring script:

cat > monitor_job.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

if [ -z "$1" ]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <job_id>"
    exit 1
fi

JOB_ID=$1
source ~/.api_credentials

echo "Monitoring job $JOB_ID..."

while true; do
    STATUS=$(curl -k -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $CONTROLLER_TOKEN" \
             https://$CONTROLLER_HOST/api/v2/jobs/$JOB_ID/ | \
             jq -r '.status')

    echo "$(date '+%H:%M:%S'): Job status is $STATUS"

    if [[ "$STATUS" == "successful" || "$STATUS" == "failed" || "$STATUS" == "error" || "$STATUS" == "canceled" ]]; then
        echo "Job completed with status: $STATUS"
        break
    fi

    sleep 5
done

echo "Final job summary:"
curl -k -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $CONTROLLER_TOKEN" \
     https://$CONTROLLER_HOST/api/v2/jobs/$JOB_ID/ | \
     jq '{id, name, status, started, finished, elapsed}'
EOF

chmod +x monitor_job.sh
Run the script against the job you launched:

./monitor_job.sh $JOB_ID
Expected output: repeated status lines (for example pending, waiting, running) followed by Job completed with status: successful, and a final JSON object with id, name, status, started, finished, and elapsed fields.

Deliverable for this subtask: terminal output showing the job transition to a terminal status (successful, failed, error, or canceled) and the final JSON summary.

Task 2: Build a Python API Client
Subtask 2.1: Create the API Client Class
Create the client script:

cat > controller_client.py << 'EOF'
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Simple Automation Controller API client for lab use.
Uses token authentication and disables SSL verification for
self-signed lab certificates (verify=False). Do not disable
SSL verification in production.
"""

import json
import os
import sys
import time

import requests
from urllib3.exceptions import InsecureRequestWarning

requests.packages.urllib3.disable_warnings(InsecureRequestWarning)


class ControllerClient:
    def __init__(self, host, token):
        self.host = host
        self.token = token
        self.base_url = f"https://{host}/api/v2"
        self.headers = {
            "Authorization": f"Bearer {token}",
            "Content-Type": "application/json",
        }

    def _get(self, endpoint, params=None):
        url = f"{self.base_url}{endpoint}"
        try:
            return requests.get(
                url, headers=self.headers, params=params, verify=False, timeout=30
            )
        except requests.exceptions.RequestException as exc:
            print(f"Request failed: {exc}")
            return None

    def _post(self, endpoint, data=None):
        url = f"{self.base_url}{endpoint}"
        try:
            return requests.post(
                url,
                headers=self.headers,
                data=json.dumps(data or {}),
                verify=False,
                timeout=30,
            )
        except requests.exceptions.RequestException as exc:
            print(f"Request failed: {exc}")
            return None

    def list_job_templates(self):
        """Return a list of job template dicts with id and name."""
        response = self._get("/job_templates/")
        if response and response.status_code == 200:
            return response.json()["results"]
        print(f"Failed to list job templates: {response.status_code if response else 'no response'}")
        return []

    def find_job_template_id(self, name):
        """Look up a job template ID by exact name match."""
        for template in self.list_job_templates():
            if template["name"] == name:
                return template["id"]
        return None

    def launch_job_template(self, template_id, extra_vars=None, limit=None):
        """Launch a job template and return the job ID, or None on failure."""
        launch_data = {}
        if extra_vars:
            launch_data["extra_vars"] = extra_vars
        if limit:
            launch_data["limit"] = limit

        response = self._post(f"/job_templates/{template_id}/launch/", data=launch_data)

        if response and response.status_code == 201:
            job_data = response.json()
            job_id = job_data.get("job", job_data.get("id"))
            print(f"Job launched successfully. Job ID: {job_id}")
            return job_id

        print(f"Failed to launch job: {response.status_code if response else 'no response'}")
        if response is not None:
            print(response.text)
        return None

    def get_job(self, job_id):
        """Return the job detail dict for a given job ID."""
        response = self._get(f"/jobs/{job_id}/")
        if response and response.status_code == 200:
            return response.json()
        print(f"Failed to get job {job_id}: {response.status_code if response else 'no response'}")
        return None

    def monitor_job(self, job_id, poll_interval=5):
        """Poll a job until it reaches a terminal status, then return its detail dict."""
        terminal_states = {"successful", "failed", "error", "canceled"}
        print(f"Monitoring job {job_id}...")

        while True:
            job = self.get_job(job_id)
            if job is None:
                return None

            status = job["status"]
            print(f"Job {job_id} status: {status}")

            if status in terminal_states:
                return job

            time.sleep(poll_interval)

    def list_recent_jobs(self, page_size=20):
        """Return the most recent jobs, ordered by most recently created first."""
        response = self._get("/jobs/", params={"order_by": "-created", "page_size": page_size})
        if response and response.status_code == 200:
            return response.json()["results"]
        print(f"Failed to list jobs: {response.status_code if response else 'no response'}")
        return []


def main():
    host = os.getenv("CONTROLLER_HOST")
    token = os.getenv("CONTROLLER_TOKEN")

    if not host or not token:
        print("Please set CONTROLLER_HOST and CONTROLLER_TOKEN environment variables")
        sys.exit(1)

    client = ControllerClient(host, token)

    print("Available job templates:")
    for template in client.list_job_templates():
        print(f"  ID: {template['id']}  Name: {template['name']}")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
EOF

chmod +x controller_client.py
Run the client to confirm it lists your templates:

source ~/.api_credentials
python3 controller_client.py
Expected output: a line Available job templates: followed by one line per template showing its ID and Name, matching what you saw with curl in Task 1.

Deliverable for this subtask: successful script execution listing at least one job template with no traceback errors.

Subtask 2.2: Launch and Monitor a Job Programmatically
Create a small driver script that uses ControllerClient to launch and monitor a job:

cat > run_job.py << 'EOF'
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import os
import sys

from controller_client import ControllerClient


def main():
    host = os.getenv("CONTROLLER_HOST")
    token = os.getenv("CONTROLLER_TOKEN")
    template_name = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else "Demo Job Template"

    if not host or not token:
        print("Please set CONTROLLER_HOST and CONTROLLER_TOKEN environment variables")
        sys.exit(1)

    client = ControllerClient(host, token)

    template_id = client.find_job_template_id(template_name)
    if template_id is None:
        print(f"Job template '{template_name}' not found")
        sys.exit(1)

    job_id = client.launch_job_template(template_id)
    if job_id is None:
        sys.exit(1)

    final_job = client.monitor_job(job_id)
    if final_job:
        print("\nFinal job details:")
        print(f"  ID: {final_job['id']}")
        print(f"  Name: {final_job['name']}")
        print(f"  Status: {final_job['status']}")
        print(f"  Started: {final_job.get('started')}")
        print(f"  Finished: {final_job.get('finished')}")
        print(f"  Elapsed: {final_job.get('elapsed')} seconds")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
EOF

chmod +x run_job.py
Run it against your demo template:

python3 run_job.py "Demo Job Template"
Expected output: Job launched successfully. Job ID: <n>, followed by repeated status lines, and a final job details block showing Status: successful (or failed/error if the underlying playbook itself fails, which still confirms the API workflow works correctly).

Deliverable for this subtask: a completed run showing the final job details block with a terminal status.

Task 3: Generate a Job Execution Summary Report
Subtask 3.1: Fetch Recent Job Data
Create a report generator script that reuses ControllerClient:

cat > generate_report.py << 'EOF'
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import os
import sys
from collections import Counter
from datetime import datetime

from controller_client import ControllerClient


class ReportGenerator:
    def __init__(self, client):
        self.client = client

    def generate_job_summary_report(self, page_size=20):
        """Print a summary report for the most recent jobs."""
        jobs = self.client.list_recent_jobs(page_size=page_size)

        if not jobs:
            print("No jobs found. Launch at least one job before running this report.")
            return

        total_jobs = len(jobs)
        status_counts = Counter(job["status"] for job in jobs)
        successful_jobs = status_counts.get("successful", 0)
        failed_jobs = status_counts.get("failed", 0) + status_counts.get("error", 0)

        success_rate = (successful_jobs / total_jobs * 100) if total_jobs else 0

        template_usage = Counter(
            job.get("name", "Unknown") for job in jobs
        )

        print("=" * 60)
        print(f"JOB EXECUTION REPORT - most recent {total_jobs} jobs")
        print("=" * 60)
        print(f"Report generated: {datetime.now().strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')}")
        print()
        print("SUMMARY:")
        print(f"  Total Jobs:      {total_jobs}")
        print(f"  Successful:      {successful_jobs}")
        print(f"  Failed/Error:    {failed_jobs}")
        print(f"  Success Rate:    {success_rate:.1f}%")
        print()
        print("STATUS BREAKDOWN:")
        for status, count in status_counts.most_common():
            print(f"  {status:<12} {count}")
        print()
        print("JOB NAME FREQUENCY:")
        for name, count in template_usage.most_common():
            print(f"  {name:<30} {count} run(s)")
        print()
        print("RECENT JOB DETAILS:")
        for job in jobs[:10]:
            print(
                f"  Job {job['id']:<6} | {job['status']:<12} | "
                f"{job.get('name', 'Unknown'):<25} | "
                f"finished: {job.get('finished') or 'N/A'}"
            )
        print("=" * 60)


def main():
    host = os.getenv("CONTROLLER_HOST")
    token = os.getenv("CONTROLLER_TOKEN")

    if not host or not token:
        print("Please set CONTROLLER_HOST and CONTROLLER_TOKEN environment variables")
        sys.exit(1)

    client = ControllerClient(host, token)
    reporter = ReportGenerator(client)
    reporter.generate_job_summary_report()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
EOF

