Troubleshooting
401 Unauthorized or 403 Forbidden responses: check that CONTROLLER_TOKEN is set correctly (echo $CONTROLLER_TOKEN) and that the token has not expired or been revoked under Users -> Tokens in the web UI.
curl or Python requests hang or time out: verify CONTROLLER_HOST is reachable with curl -k -I https://$CONTROLLER_HOST/api/v2/ping/ and check that no firewall or proxy is blocking port 443.
Conclusion
In this lab you worked directly with the Automation Controller REST API using both curl and Python, covering the core operations of launching job templates, polling job status until completion, and listing recent job history. You built a reusable ControllerClient class that centralizes authentication and error handling, then used it to drive a job from launch to a validated summary report, cross-checking the API output against the Automation Controller web UI. Along the way you practiced secure token handling by storing credentials in a permission-restricted file and learned why disabling SSL verification with -k or verify=False is acceptable only in an isolated lab setting, never in production. For production use, replace token-in-file storage with a secrets manager or vault integration, enable proper CA-signed certificate verification, and consult the official Ansible Automation Platform Controller API Guide and the browsable API at /api/v2/ on your own controller for the complete set of endpoints beyond those covered here.
